WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.920
Part 4. You will hear a presentation by a food science student about the production of maple syrup.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.920
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:50.000
First, you will hear a presentation by a food science student about the production of maple syrup.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Hello, everyone. Today we're going to look at another natural food product, and that's maple syrup.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
What is this exactly? Well, maple syrup looks rather like clear honey, but it's not made by bees.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
It's produced from the plant fluid, or sap, inside the maple tree, and that makes maple syrup a very natural product.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Maple syrup is a thick, golden, sweet-tasting liquid that can be bought in bottles or jars

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
and poured onto foods such as waffles and ice cream or used in the baking of cakes and pastries.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:03.000
It contains no preservatives or added ingredients, and it provides a healthy alternative to refined sugar.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Let's just talk a bit about the maple tree itself, which is where maple syrup comes from.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:21.000
So, there are many species of maple tree, and they'll grow without fertilizer in areas where there's plenty of moisture in the soil.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.000
However, they'll only do this if another important criterion is fulfilled,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:38.000
which is that they must have full or partial sun exposure during the day and very cool nights, and I'll talk more about that in a minute.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.000
There are only certain parts of the world that provide all these conditions.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:53.000
One is Canada, and by that I mean all parts of Canada, and the other is the northeastern states of North America.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:58.000
In these areas, the climate suits the trees perfectly.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
In fact, Canada produces over two-thirds of the world's maple syrup,

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:12.000
which is why the five-pointed maple leaf is a Canadian symbol and has featured on the flag since 1964.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
So, how did maple syrup production begin?

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Well, long before Europeans settled in these parts of the world, the indigenous communities had started producing maple sugar.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:36.000
They bored holes in the trunks of maple trees and used containers made of tree bark to collect the liquid sap as it poured out.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:44.000
As they were unable to keep the liquid for any length of time, they didn't have storage facilities in those days,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.000
they boiled the liquid by placing pieces of rock that had become scorching hot from the sun into the sap.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:01.000
They did this until it turned into sugar, and they were then able to use this to sweeten their food and drinks.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Since that time, improvements have been made to the process, but it has changed very little overall.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.000
So, let's look at the production of maple syrup today.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.000
Clearly, the maple forests are a valuable resource in many Canadian and North American communities.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:36.000
The trees have to be well looked after, and they cannot be used to make syrup until the trunks reach a diameter of around 25 centimeters.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.000
This can take anything up to 40 years.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:48.000
As I've already mentioned, maple trees need the right conditions to grow and also to produce sap.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Why is this?

00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Well, what happens is that during a cold night, the tree absorbs water from the soil, and that rises through the tree's vascular system.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:10.000
But then, in the warmer daytime, the change in temperature causes the water to be pushed back down to the bottom of the tree.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:19.000
This continual movement, up and down, leads to the formation of the sap needed for maple syrup production.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:31.000
When the tree is ready, it can be tapped, and this involves drilling a small hole into the trunk and inserting a tube into it that ends in a bucket.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:42.000
The trees can often take several taps, though the workers take care not to cause any damage to the healthy growth of the tree itself.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:51.000
The sap that comes out of the trees consists of 98% water and 2% sugar and other nutrients.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:06:02.000
It has to be boiled so that much of that water evaporates, and this process has to take place immediately using what are called evaporators.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.000
These are basically extremely large pans.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.000
The sap is poured into these, a fire is built, and the pans are then heated until the sap boils.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:20.000
As it does this, the water evaporates and the syrup begins to form.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:28.000
The evaporation process creates large quantities of steam, and the sap becomes thicker and denser,

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:37.000
and at just the right moment, when the sap is thick enough to be called maple syrup, the worker removes it from the heat.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:50.000
After this process, something called sugar sand has to be filtered out as this builds up during the boiling and gives the syrup a cloudy appearance and a slightly gritty taste.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:58.000
Once this has been done, the syrup is ready to be packaged so that it can be used for a whole variety of products.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:09.000
It takes 40 litres of sap to produce 1 litre of maple syrup, so you can get an idea of how much is needed.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.000
So, that's the basic process.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:25.000
That is the end of Part 4. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 4.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Thank you.

